## The ruling

Owner ruling 2026-08-27: **in**.

The catalogue header excluded `std::os::*` as *"raw-fd extensions"*. That noun is correct for what it names, and **that part stands** — `AsRawFd` / `FromRawFd` / `IntoRawFd` hand over a descriptor number and reach no syscall. But the exclusion was being read as covering the whole namespace, and those modules also carry plain filesystem mutation.

## The asymmetry that motivated it

`std::fs::soft_link` — the cross-platform way to create a symlink — **was** catalogued, while `std::os::unix::fs::symlink` was not. So the tool flagged one way of creating a symlink and ignored the other, depending purely on which API the program reached for. And a Linux program calling `chroot` or `chown` through this module read as doing nothing at all there: a false all-clear, the error direction this project treats as worst.

A test now pins all three symlink spellings, so the asymmetry cannot come back quietly.

